TETRIS for CHGame: independently implemented single-player adaptation.
Tetris name, game and original presentation: their respective rights holders.

Gameplay reference: Nintendo's 1989 Game Boy Tetris instruction booklet:
https://www.videogamemanual.com/gameboy/Tetris%20(USA).pdf
Rotation, gravity, repeat and randomizer research:
https://github.com/kaspermeerts/tetris
The DMG title art, font, block patterns and screen maps are converted from
the published reference atlases in that repository, revision:
b95c66859339f5523e80213de5857eefc1c7703f
Original graphical artwork: Nintendo, 1989, and its respective rights holders.
Source URLs and SHA-256 hashes are in references/dmg/sources.json.
The BSD software license does not relicense the original graphical artwork.
No Game Boy ROM or Nintendo executable binary is bundled.

Visual checks also used the DMG title capture at:
https://images.nintendolife.com/00b6d3e81d84b/tetris-2.900x.jpg
and NintendoComplete's recorded playthrough, title and gameplay frames:
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BQwohHgrk2s

Original DMG A/B/C music score data, pitch tables, wave patterns and playback
behavior are referenced from music.asm, music_macros.asm and audio.asm in the
pinned disassembly above. Source URLs and SHA-256 hashes: tools/music.json.
A: traditional Korobeiniki, arranged by Hirokazu Tanaka.
B: original composition by Hirokazu Tanaka.
C: J. S. Bach, French Suite No. 3 in B minor, BWV 814, Menuet, arranged for
Game Boy by Hirokazu Tanaka. Original score/arrangement rights are retained
by their respective rights holders and are not covered by the software license.
The original scores are interpreted as data, without Nintendo executable code.
The native PWM output and cue mixer are derived from the ATM
Soundtracks demo at https://github.com/juliendorra/chgame-soundtracks.
Game sound cues are authored for this adaptation.
The software license does not relicense the music or trademarks.

